The plan is written to session_plan/ (two task files plus issue_responses.md). The commit step did nothing: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so the files exist on disk but aren't committed. I assume the harness reads them from disk; the commit command already allowed for failure with || true.

Task 1 (product): `/spawn` no longer force-removes a worktree that still holds uncommitted work.
• The bug, read from code but not yet run: when a worker's handoff commit fails (a pre-commit hook rejects it, signing fails, no user.email is set), cleanup runs git worktree remove --force and the worker's uncommitted changes are lost. The cleanup after a failed output-file write, and the hourly stale sweep, can lose work the same way. Claude Code 2.1.304 fixed the same kind of bug.
• The fix: the guard goes inside the one cleanup function, so all three callers get it. A worktree with uncommitted changes, or one whose git status fails, is kept, and a line names its path and how to recover or discard it. A clean worktree is removed exactly as today.
• Before the fix, the task reproduces the loss in a scratch repo.
• After it, the task runs a positive control and greps src/ for every worktree remove, remove_dir_all and branch -D, and lists them in the commit.
• One warning in the task: commands_spawn.rs is already over its size limit in tests/module_size.rs, so the guard may need to move into a new src/spawn_worktree.rs.

Task 2 (product, backlog drain): close #982's remaining items.
• Bare yoyo run and tree/undo with a bad argument will exit 2, like the other usage errors.
• yoyo grep with no match will exit 1, because grep, rg and git grep all do. yoyo find with no match stays 0, because find(1) does. I settled that open question by looking up what those tools do, and the task asks for one command each to confirm it.
• Before the push, the task rewrites user-facing #982 pointers, such as the last sentence of the exit-codes paragraph in docs/src/usage/commands.md.
• The commit title says "Fixes #982" only if all three changes land. Otherwise it says "Part of #982" and the task posts a comment on #982 naming what landed.

